explanation74":"Saint Peter [left panel] (c. 1360) is catalogued as a work by Nardo di Cione."},{"id":"EXP77-3625","nodeId":"EXP77-3625","anchor":"NGA61-33746","subject":"Love the Sentinel","value":"Jean Honoré Fragonard","group":"Jean Honoré Fragonard","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"artist","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.33746.html"}],"explanation74":"Love the Sentinel (c. 1773/1776) is catalogued as a work by Jean Honoré Fragonard."},{"id":"EXP77-3626","nodeId":"EXP77-3626","anchor":"NGA61-166472","subject":"En route pour la pêche (Setting Out to Fish)","value":"John Singer Sargent","group":"John Singer Sargent","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"artist","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.166472.html"}],"explanation74":"En route pour la pêche (Setting Out to Fish) (1878) is catalogued as a work by John Singer Sargent."},{"id":"EXP77-3627","nodeId":"EXP77-3627","anchor":"NGA61-46087","subject":"Master John Heathcote","value":"National Gallery of Art, Washington","group":"Thomas Gainsborough","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"collection","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.46087.html"}],"explanation74":"The collection record places this work in National Gallery of Art, Washington."},{"id":"EXP77-3628","nodeId":"EXP77-3628","anchor":"NGA61-46602","subject":"Christopher Columbus and His Son at La Rábida","value":"Eugène Delacroix","group":"Eugène Delacroix","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"artist","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.46602.html"}],"explanation74":"Christopher Columbus and His Son at La Rábida (1838) is catalogued as a work by Eugène Delacroix."},{"id":"EXP77-3629","nodeId":"EXP77-3629","anchor":"NGA61-203","subject":"Cupids Disarming Sleeping Nymphs","value":"Giuseppe Maria Crespi","group":"Giuseppe Maria Crespi","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"artist","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.203.html"}],"explanation74":"Cupids Disarming Sleeping Nymphs (c. 1690/1705) is catalogued as a work by Giuseppe Maria Crespi."},{"id":"EXP77-3630","nodeId":"EXP77-3630","anchor":"NGA61-103611","subject":"View near Naples","value":"Simon Denis","group":"Simon Denis","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"artist","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.103611.html"}],"explanation74":"View near Naples (c. 1806) is catalogued as a work by Simon Denis."},{"id":"EXP77-3631","nodeId":"EXP77-3631","anchor":"ART57-MET-436488","subject":"Tobias and the Angel","value":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","group":"Davide Ghirlandaio (David Bigordi)","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"collection","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436488"}],"explanation74":"The collection record places this work in The Metropolitan Museum of Art."},{"id":"EXP77-3632","nodeId":"EXP77-3632","anchor":"NGA61-372","subject":"Susanna","value":"Domenico Tintoretto","group":"Domenico Tintoretto","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"artist","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.372.html"}],"explanation74":"Susanna (c. 1580s) is catalogued as a work by Domenico Tintoretto."},{"id":"EXP77-3633","nodeId":"EXP77-3633","anchor":"NGA61-182","subject":"The Presentation of the Virgin in the Temple","value":"Andrea di Bartolo","group":"Andrea di Bartolo","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"artist","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.182.html"}],"explanation74":"The Presentation of the Virgin in the Temple (c. 1400/1405) is catalogued as a work by Andrea di Bartolo."},{"id":"EXP77-3634","nodeId":"EXP77-3634","anchor":"NGA61-46584","subject":"Forest of Fontainebleau","value":"Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot","group":"Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"artist","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.46584.html"}],"explanation74":"Forest of Fontainebleau (1834) is catalogued as a work by Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot."},{"id":"EXP77-3635","nodeId":"EXP77-3635","anchor":"NGA61-93000","subject":"Self-Portrait, Aged 21","value":"National Gallery of Art, Washington","group":"Edouard Vuillard","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"collection","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.93000.html"}],"explanation74":"The collection record places this work in National Gallery of Art, Washington."},{"id":"EXP77-3636","nodeId":"EXP77-3636","anchor":"NGA61-893","subject":"Saint Catherine of Alexandria, with an Angel [right panel]","value":"Pietro Lorenzetti","group":"Pietro Lorenzetti","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"artist","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.893.html"}],"explanation74":"Saint Catherine of Alexandria, with an Angel [right panel] (probably 1340) is catalogued as a work by Pietro Lorenzetti."},{"id":"EXP77-3637","nodeId":"EXP77-3637","anchor":"NGA61-1160","subject":"The Hon. Mrs. Thomas Graham","value":"Thomas Gainsborough","group":"Thomas Gainsborough","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"artist","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.1160.html"}],"explanation74":"The Hon. Mrs. Thomas Graham (c. 1775/1777) is catalogued as a work by Thomas Gainsborough."},{"id":"EXP77-3638","nodeId":"EXP77-3638","anchor":"NGA61-210038","subject":"Dunes by the Sea","value":"Jacob van Ruisdael","group":"Jacob van Ruisdael","major":"Art History","era":"Other","relation":"artist","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.210038.html"}],"explanation74":"Dunes by the Sea (1648) is catalogued as a work by Jacob van Ruisdael."},{"id":"EXP77-3639","nodeId":"EXP77-3639","anchor":"MET44-437431","subject":"Hills around the Bay of Moulin Huet, Guernsey","value":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","group":"Pierre-Auguste Renoir","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"collection","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/437431"}],"explanation74":"The collection record places this work in The Metropolitan Museum of Art."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3640","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3640","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Squinch","value":"Squinch","group":"Squinch","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Squinch","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Squinch"}],"explanation74":"A squinch bridges a corner; a pendentive is a curved triangular surface."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3641","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3641","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Muqarnas","value":"Muqarnas","group":"Muqarnas","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Muqarnas","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muqarnas"}],"explanation74":"Muqarnas breaks a surface into repeated small cellular forms."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3642","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3642","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Iwan","value":"Iwan","group":"Iwan","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Iwan","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iwan"}],"explanation74":"An iwan is enclosed on three sides and open on the fourth."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3643","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3643","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q162","subject":"Mihrab","value":"Direction of Mecca","group":"Mihrab","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"function","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Mihrab","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mihrab"}],"explanation74":"The mihrab marks the qibla, the direction faced during prayer."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3644","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3644","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Minbar","value":"Minbar","group":"Minbar","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Minbar","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minbar"}],"explanation74":"The minbar is a pulpit; the mihrab indicates the direction of prayer."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3645","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3645","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Compluvium","value":"Compluvium","group":"Compluvium","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Compluvium","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cavaedium"}],"explanation74":"Rain entered through the compluvium and could collect in the impluvium below."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3646","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3646","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Triclinium","value":"Dining","group":"Triclinium","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"function","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Triclinium","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triclinium"}],"explanation74":"A triclinium was a dining room, named after its arrangement of reclining couches."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3647","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3647","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Exedra","value":"Exedra","group":"Exedra","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Exedra","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exedra"}],"explanation74":"An exedra provides a recessed space for sitting or conversation."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3648","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3648","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q038","subject":"Nymphaeum","value":"Water and nymphs","group":"Nymphaeum","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"function","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Nymphaeum","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nymphaeum"}],"explanation74":"Nymphaea were monuments associated with nymphs and commonly incorporated fountains or springs."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3649","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3649","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q069","subject":"Cryptoporticus","value":"A covered passage","group":"Cryptoporticus","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cryptoporticus","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cryptoporticus"}],"explanation74":"A cryptoporticus is a covered gallery or passage, often partly underground."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3650","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3650","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Spolia","value":"Spolia","group":"Spolia","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Spolia","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spolia"}],"explanation74":"Spolia are earlier building materials or artworks incorporated into later constructions."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3651","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3651","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q737","subject":"Opus reticulatum","value":"Opus reticulatum","group":"Opus reticulatum","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Opus reticulatum","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opus_reticulatum"}],"explanation74":"Opus reticulatum is recognizable by its regular diamond-shaped mesh."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3652","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3652","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q737","subject":"Opus incertum","value":"Opus incertum","group":"Opus incertum","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Opus incertum","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opus_incertum"}],"explanation74":"The irregular stone facing distinguishes opus incertum from regular net-pattern masonry."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3653","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3653","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q737","subject":"Opus spicatum","value":"Herringbone","group":"Opus spicatum","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"pattern","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Opus spicatum","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opus_spicatum"}],"explanation74":"Opus spicatum arranges units diagonally in a herringbone pattern."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3654","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3654","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Ashlar","value":"Regularly dressed stone blocks","group":"Ashlar","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ashlar","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashlar"}],"explanation74":"Ashlar uses carefully cut blocks laid with relatively regular joints."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3655","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3655","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q548","subject":"Solomonic column","value":"A spiralling shaft","group":"Solomonic column","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Solomonic column","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solomonic_column"}],"explanation74":"A Solomonic column has a helical or twisted shaft."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3656","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3656","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q098","subject":"Diocletian window","value":"Thermal window","group":"Diocletian window","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Diocletian window","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diocletian_window"}],"explanation74":"Thermal or Diocletian windows derive their name from Roman bath architecture."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3657","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3657","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q089","subject":"Piano nobile","value":"Principal reception floor","group":"Piano nobile","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Piano nobile","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_nobile"}],"explanation74":"The piano nobile contains the principal reception rooms, generally above the ground floor."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3658","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3658","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q265","subject":"Mezzanine","value":"An intermediate floor","group":"Mezzanine","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Mezzanine","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mezzanine"}],"explanation74":"A mezzanine is an intermediate level between the main floors of a building."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3659","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3659","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q265","subject":"Loggia","value":"An open colonnaded side","group":"Loggia","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Loggia","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loggia"}],"explanation74":"A loggia is a roofed gallery open along one or more sides."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3660","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3660","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q124","subject":"Ha-ha","value":"Barrier without blocking the view","group":"Ha-ha","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"function","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ha-ha","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ha-ha"}],"explanation74":"A recessed boundary keeps animals out while preserving an uninterrupted landscape view."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3661","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3661","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q069","subject":"Stoa","value":"A covered colonnaded walkway","group":"Stoa","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Stoa","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa"}],"explanation74":"Stoas provided covered public space for movement, trade and social activity."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3662","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3662","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Tetrapylon","value":"Four-way monumental gateway","group":"Tetrapylon","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Tetrapylon","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetrapylon"}],"explanation74":"Tetrapyla commonly marked important intersections with four openings or groups of supports."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3663","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3663","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q069","subject":"Coffer","value":"A recessed ceiling panel","group":"Coffer","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Coffer","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffer"}],"explanation74":"Coffering divides a ceiling or vault into recessed panels."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3664","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3664","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Voussoir","value":"Voussoir","group":"Voussoir","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Voussoir","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voussoir"}],"explanation74":"Voussoirs form the curved arch ring; the central upper voussoir is the keystone."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3665","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3665","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Intrados","value":"Intrados","group":"Intrados","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Intrados","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intrados"}],"explanation74":"The intrados is the inner surface; the extrados is the outer curve."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3666","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3666","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Spandrel","value":"Spandrel","group":"Spandrel","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Spandrel","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spandrel"}],"explanation74":"Spandrels occupy the spaces above and beside the curve of an arch."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3667","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3667","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Centring","value":"Centring","group":"Centring","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"function","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Centring","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centring"}],"explanation74":"Centring holds the units in place until the completed arch can support itself."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3668","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3668","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q004","subject":"Fan vault","value":"English Perpendicular Gothic","group":"Fan vault","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"classification","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Fan vault","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fan_vault"}],"explanation74":"Fan vaulting develops conical, fan-like groups of ribs in English late Gothic architecture."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3669","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3669","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q623","subject":"Tensile structure","value":"Tension","group":"Tensile structure","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"mechanism","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Tensile structure","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tensile_structure"}],"explanation74":"Flexible cables carry tensile forces and cannot act as rigid compression members."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3670","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3670","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Catenary","value":"Catenary","group":"Catenary","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Catenary","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catenary"}],"explanation74":"A freely hanging uniform chain takes the form of a catenary."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3671","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3671","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q216","subject":"Diagrid","value":"A network of diagonal members","group":"Diagrid","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Diagrid","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diagrid"}],"explanation74":"A diagrid uses interconnected diagonal structural members."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3672","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3672","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q547","subject":"Space frame","value":"A three-dimensional structural framework","group":"Space frame","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Space frame","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_frame"}],"explanation74":"A space frame distributes loads through an interconnected three-dimensional framework."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3673","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3673","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Brise soleil","value":"Solar shading","group":"Brise soleil","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"function","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Brise soleil","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brise_soleil"}],"explanation74":"A brise-soleil reduces unwanted direct sunlight at a building's facade."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3674","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3674","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q243","subject":"Trombe wall","value":"Passive solar heating","group":"Trombe wall","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"function","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Trombe wall","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trombe_wall"}],"explanation74":"A glazed, heat-storing wall collects solar energy and releases heat into the building."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3675","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3675","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q738","subject":"Windcatcher","value":"Natural ventilation","group":"Windcatcher","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"function","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Windcatcher","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windcatcher"}],"explanation74":"Windcatchers channel air and support natural ventilation, particularly in hot climates."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3676","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3676","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q038","subject":"Wattle and daub","value":"Woven branches and earthen plaster","group":"Wattle and daub","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"materials","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Wattle and daub","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wattle_and_daub"}],"explanation74":"A woven framework is coated with an earthen mixture to form the wall."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3677","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3677","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Rammed earth","value":"Rammed earth","group":"Rammed earth","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"process","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Rammed earth","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rammed_earth"}],"explanation74":"Rammed-earth walls are made by compacting successive layers of an earth mixture."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3678","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3678","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Adobe","value":"Sun-dried earthen brick","group":"Adobe","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"materials","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Adobe","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe"}],"explanation74":"Adobe bricks are shaped from an earthen mixture and dried without firing."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3679","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3679","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q724","subject":"Balloon framing","value":"More than one storey","group":"Balloon framing","major":"Architecture","era":"Early 20th century","relation":"structure","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Balloon framing","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Framing_%28construction%29"}],"explanation74":"Continuous long studs distinguish balloon framing from storey-by-storey platform framing."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3680","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3680","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q366","subject":"Dom-Ino House","value":"Enclosing walls","group":"Dom-Ino House","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"structure","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Dom-Ino House","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dom-Ino_House"}],"explanation74":"Its reinforced-concrete frame allowed more freedom in the design of walls and plans."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3681","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3681","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q005","subject":"Plan Voisin","value":"Paris","group":"Plan Voisin","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"location","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Plan Voisin","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plan_Voisin"}],"explanation74":"The 1925 proposal imagined rebuilding a large area of central Paris."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3682","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3682","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q411","subject":"Garden city movement","value":"Ebenezer Howard","group":"Garden city movement","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Garden city movement","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_city_movement"}],"explanation74":"Howard proposed planned settlements combining urban opportunities with rural advantages."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3683","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3683","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q657","subject":"Garden city movement","value":"A surrounding green belt","group":"Garden city movement","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"planning","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Garden city movement","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_city_movement"}],"explanation74":"The green belt preserves countryside and helps contain the planned settlement."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3684","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3684","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q565","subject":"Athens Charter","value":"Four","group":"Athens Charter","major":"Architecture","era":"20th century","relation":"classification","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Athens Charter","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athens_Charter"}],"explanation74":"The functions were dwelling, work, recreation and circulation."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3685","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3685","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q411","subject":"City Beautiful movement","value":"City Beautiful","group":"City Beautiful movement","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"classification","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: City Beautiful movement","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Beautiful_movement"}],"explanation74":"City Beautiful linked civic improvement to grand public buildings and ordered urban spaces."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3686","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3686","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q006","subject":"Metabolism (architecture)","value":"Metabolism","group":"Metabolism (architecture)","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"classification","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Metabolism (architecture)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metabolism_%28architecture%29"}],"explanation74":"Japanese Metabolism explored expandable systems and replaceable building components."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3687","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3687","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q359","subject":"Nakagin Capsule Tower","value":"Replaceable prefabricated capsules","group":"Nakagin Capsule Tower","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"structure","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Nakagin Capsule Tower","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nakagin_Capsule_Tower"}],"explanation74":"Kurokawa's design attached prefabricated living capsules to service cores."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3688","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3688","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Centre Georges Pompidou","value":"Air circulation","group":"Centre Georges Pompidou","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"services","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Centre Georges Pompidou","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centre_Georges_Pompidou"}],"explanation74":"The building's colour coding identifies blue with air-conditioning and ventilation systems."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3689","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3689","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q009","subject":"Lloyd's building","value":"Services placed outside","group":"Lloyd's building","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"structure","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Lloyd's building","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lloyd%27s_building"}],"explanation74":"External service towers free the interior and express the building's organization."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3690","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3690","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q366","subject":"Farnsworth House","value":"Glass walls","group":"Farnsworth House","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"materials","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Farnsworth House","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farnsworth_House"}],"explanation74":"The house is defined by a steel frame and extensive transparent glazing."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3691","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3691","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q129","subject":"Villa Savoye","value":"A ramp","group":"Villa Savoye","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"circulation","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Villa Savoye","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Savoye"}],"explanation74":"The ramp links the levels and organizes a changing sequence of views."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3692","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3692","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q008","subject":"Bauhaus","value":"Hannes Meyer","group":"Bauhaus","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"succession","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Bauhaus","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bauhaus"}],"explanation74":"Meyer directed the school before Mies van der Rohe's appointment in 1930."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3693","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3693","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q068","subject":"Glass Pavilion","value":"Deutscher Werkbund exhibition","group":"Glass Pavilion","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Glass Pavilion","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass_Pavilion"}],"explanation74":"The pavilion was an experimental glass building at the Cologne Werkbund exhibition."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3694","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3694","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q359","subject":"Einstein Tower","value":"Expressionism","group":"Einstein Tower","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"style","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Einstein Tower","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Einstein_Tower"}],"explanation74":"Its sculptural, fluid form is a landmark of Expressionist architecture."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3695","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3695","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q069","subject":"Il Redentore","value":"A plague epidemic","group":"Il Redentore","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Il Redentore","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Il_Redentore"}],"explanation74":"The votive church followed Venice's plague epidemic of 1575–1576."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3696","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3696","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"San Giorgio Maggiore (church)","value":"Overlapping temple fronts","group":"San Giorgio Maggiore (church)","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"composition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: San Giorgio Maggiore (church)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://homepages.bluffton.edu/~sullivanm/giorgiom/giorgiom.html"}],"explanation74":"Superimposed classical temple fronts address the different heights of nave and aisles."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3697","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3697","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q002","subject":"Sant'Andrea, Mantua","value":"Triumphal arch","group":"Sant'Andrea, Mantua","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"composition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Sant'Andrea, Mantua","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sant%27Andrea%2C_Mantua"}],"explanation74":"The central arched opening adapts the language of the Roman triumphal arch."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3698","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3698","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q293","subject":"San Pietro in Montorio","value":"San Pietro in Montorio","group":"San Pietro in Montorio","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"location","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: San Pietro in Montorio","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Pietro_in_Montorio"}],"explanation74":"The circular Tempietto stands in the courtyard of San Pietro in Montorio."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3699","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3699","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q111","subject":"Palazzo della Civiltà Italiana","value":"EUR","group":"Palazzo della Civiltà Italiana","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Palazzo della Civiltà Italiana","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palazzo_della_Civilt%C3%A0_Italiana"}],"explanation74":"It was designed for the planned 1942 exposition in the EUR district."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3700","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3700","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q422","subject":"Sagrada Família","value":"A forest canopy","group":"Sagrada Família","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"structure","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Sagrada Família","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sagrada_Fam%C3%ADlia"}],"explanation74":"The branching columns create a tree-like structural and spatial system."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3701","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3701","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q038","subject":"Fagus Factory","value":"Glazed corners and curtain walls","group":"Fagus Factory","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"innovation","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Fagus Factory","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fagus_Factory"}],"explanation74":"Its extensive glazing and light corners challenged the appearance of massive industrial walls."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3702","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3702","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q017","subject":"Villa Mairea","value":"Finland","group":"Villa Mairea","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"location","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Villa Mairea","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Mairea"}],"explanation74":"Villa Mairea stands in Noormarkku, Finland."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3703","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3703","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q679","subject":"Church of the Light","value":"A cross","group":"Church of the Light","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"form","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Church of the Light","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_the_Light"}],"explanation74":"The cruciform opening makes light a central element of the church interior."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3704","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3704","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q009","subject":"Jewish Museum Berlin","value":"Voids","group":"Jewish Museum Berlin","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"concept","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Jewish Museum Berlin","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jewish_Museum_Berlin"}],"explanation74":"The voids give architectural form to absence and loss."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3705","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3705","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q162","subject":"Hagia Sophia","value":"Conquest of Constantinople","group":"Hagia Sophia","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Hagia Sophia","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hagia_Sophia"}],"explanation74":"The conversion followed the Ottoman capture of Constantinople in 1453."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3706","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3706","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Alhambra","value":"Nasrid","group":"Alhambra","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"patronage","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Alhambra","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alhambra"}],"explanation74":"The Nasrid rulers made Granada the centre of their late medieval kingdom."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3707","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3707","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q598","subject":"Lost-wax casting","value":"A wax model","group":"Lost-wax casting","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"process","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Lost-wax casting","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lost-wax_casting"}],"explanation74":"A refractory mould is formed around wax, which is removed before the metal is poured."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3708","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3708","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q031","subject":"Repoussé and chasing","value":"Repoussé","group":"Repoussé and chasing","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"technique","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Repoussé and chasing","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Repouss%C3%A9_and_chasing"}],"explanation74":"Repoussé shapes the metal from behind; chasing refines details from the front."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3709","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3709","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q233","subject":"Chryselephantine sculpture","value":"Gold and ivory","group":"Chryselephantine sculpture","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"materials","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Chryselephantine sculpture","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chryselephantine_sculpture"}],"explanation74":"The term describes sculpture using gold and ivory, especially major ancient Greek cult statues."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3710","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3710","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q734","subject":"Kore (sculpture)","value":"A young clothed woman","group":"Kore (sculpture)","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"identification","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Kore (sculpture)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kore_%28sculpture%29"}],"explanation74":"A kore is a standing young female figure, generally clothed."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3711","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3711","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Archaic smile","value":"Archaic smile","group":"Archaic smile","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"term","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Archaic smile","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archaic_smile"}],"explanation74":"The Archaic smile is a stylized feature rather than a portrait of a particular emotion."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3712","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3712","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Aphrodite of Knidos","value":"Monumental nude female figure","group":"Aphrodite of Knidos","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"innovation","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Aphrodite of Knidos","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aphrodite_of_Knidos"}],"explanation74":"The statue became a celebrated model for the nude female figure in Greek sculpture."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3713","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3713","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q449","subject":"Parthenon Frieze","value":"Panathenaic procession","group":"Parthenon Frieze","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"iconography","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Parthenon Frieze","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parthenon_Frieze"}],"explanation74":"The usual interpretation connects the frieze with the Athenian festival honouring Athena."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3714","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3714","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q294","subject":"Pergamon Altar","value":"Battle of gods and giants","group":"Pergamon Altar","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"iconography","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Pergamon Altar","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pergamon_Altar"}],"explanation74":"The Gigantomachy shows the Olympian gods fighting the giants."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3715","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3715","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q031","subject":"Laocoön and His Sons","value":"Serpents","group":"Laocoön and His Sons","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"iconography","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Laocoön and His Sons","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laoco%C3%B6n_and_His_Sons"}],"explanation74":"The Trojan priest and his sons are entangled by serpents in the sculptural group."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3716","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3716","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Augustus of Prima Porta","value":"Roman military standards","group":"Augustus of Prima Porta","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"iconography","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Augustus of Prima Porta","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustus_of_Prima_Porta"}],"explanation74":"The relief is commonly interpreted as the return of Roman standards by the Parthians."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3717","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3717","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Ara Pacis","value":"Peace","group":"Ara Pacis","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"dedication","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ara Pacis","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ara_Pacis"}],"explanation74":"The Altar of Augustan Peace connected the emperor's rule with peace and prosperity."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3718","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3718","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Equestrian Statue of Marcus Aurelius","value":"Constantine","group":"Equestrian Statue of Marcus Aurelius","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"reception","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Equestrian Statue of Marcus Aurelius","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equestrian_statue_of_Marcus_Aurelius"}],"explanation74":"It was believed to portray the Christian emperor Constantine rather than a pagan ruler."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3719","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3719","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Donatello","value":"Freestanding male nude","group":"Donatello","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"form","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Donatello","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donatello"}],"explanation74":"The bronze statue revived the freestanding nude male figure associated with antiquity."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3720","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3720","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q332","subject":"Non finito","value":"An unfinished treatment","group":"Non finito","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"term","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Non finito","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non_finito"}],"explanation74":"Rough or unfinished areas can remain visibly part of a sculptural work."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3721","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3721","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q152","subject":"David (Michelangelo)","value":"Florence Cathedral","group":"David (Michelangelo)","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"original context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: David (Michelangelo)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_%28Michelangelo%29"}],"explanation74":"The statue was intended for the cathedral project before being placed beside Palazzo Vecchio."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3722","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3722","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q233","subject":"Apollo and Daphne (Bernini)","value":"Ovid's Metamorphoses","group":"Apollo and Daphne (Bernini)","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"literary source","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Apollo and Daphne (Bernini)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_and_Daphne_%28Bernini%29"}],"explanation74":"Ovid recounts Daphne's transformation into a laurel tree while escaping Apollo."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3723","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3723","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Ecstasy of Saint Teresa","value":"Cornaro Chapel","group":"Ecstasy of Saint Teresa","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"setting","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ecstasy of Saint Teresa","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecstasy_of_Saint_Teresa"}],"explanation74":"The Cornaro Chapel combines sculpture, architecture, light and theatrical spectators."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3724","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3724","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Venus Victrix (Canova)","value":"Venus","group":"Venus Victrix (Canova)","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"iconography","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Venus Victrix (Canova)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus_Victrix_%28Canova%29"}],"explanation74":"The reclining figure identifies Pauline with the victorious Venus."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3725","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3725","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"The Burghers of Calais","value":"Hundred Years' War","group":"The Burghers of Calais","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"historical context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: The Burghers of Calais","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Burghers_of_Calais"}],"explanation74":"The group recalls citizens offering themselves after the English siege of Calais in 1347."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3726","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3726","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q031","subject":"The Thinker","value":"Dante","group":"The Thinker","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"iconography","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: The Thinker","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Thinker"}],"explanation74":"The figure was connected to Dante contemplating the scene of The Gates of Hell."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3727","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3727","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q643","subject":"Bird in Space","value":"The movement of flight","group":"Bird in Space","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"formal analysis","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Bird in Space","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird_in_Space"}],"explanation74":"Its elongated, streamlined form abstracts the idea of flight."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3728","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3728","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q242","subject":"Fountain (Duchamp)","value":"R. Mutt","group":"Fountain (Duchamp)","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"inscription","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Fountain (Duchamp)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fountain_%28Duchamp%29"}],"explanation74":"The signature R. Mutt formed part of the work's challenge to artistic authorship and institutions."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3729","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3729","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q316","subject":"L.H.O.O.Q.","value":"Mona Lisa","group":"L.H.O.O.Q.","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"visual relation","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: L.H.O.O.Q.","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L.H.O.O.Q."}],"explanation74":"Duchamp added a moustache and beard to a reproduction of Leonardo's painting."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3730","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3730","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q152","subject":"Artist's Shit","value":"Sealed tin cans","group":"Artist's Shit","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"object","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Artist's Shit","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artist%27s_Shit"}],"explanation74":"The work uses commodity packaging to question artistic value and authorship."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3731","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3731","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q598","subject":"Venus of the Rags","value":"A pile of discarded clothes","group":"Venus of the Rags","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"composition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Venus of the Rags","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus_of_the_Rags"}],"explanation74":"The work confronts an idealized classical image with used, everyday material."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3732","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3732","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Jannis Kounellis","value":"Live horses","group":"Jannis Kounellis","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"installation","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Jannis Kounellis","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jannis_Kounellis"}],"explanation74":"The horses made living bodies and the gallery environment part of the work."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3733","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3733","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q233","subject":"Combine painting","value":"Painting and sculpture","group":"Combine painting","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"form","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Combine painting","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combine_painting"}],"explanation74":"Combines incorporate three-dimensional objects into painted structures."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3734","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3734","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q146","subject":"The Factory","value":"Andy Warhol","group":"The Factory","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: The Factory","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Factory"}],"explanation74":"Warhol's studio was a centre of artistic production and collaboration."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3735","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3735","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q311","subject":"Ben-Day dots","value":"Commercial printing","group":"Ben-Day dots","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"technique","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ben-Day dots","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ben-Day_dots"}],"explanation74":"Ben-Day dots evoke the mechanical reproduction of colour in printed images."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3736","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3736","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q132","subject":"Color field","value":"Colour Field painting","group":"Color field","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"classification","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Color field","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_field"}],"explanation74":"Colour Field painting emphasizes expansive fields of colour."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3737","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3737","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Barnett Newman","value":"Zips","group":"Barnett Newman","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"term","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Barnett Newman","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barnett_Newman"}],"explanation74":"Newman's vertical bands organize the pictorial field and are known as zips."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3738","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3738","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q093","subject":"Black Square","value":"Religious icons","group":"Black Square","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"exhibition context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Black Square","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Square"}],"explanation74":"The placement invoked the traditional icon corner and emphasized the work's significance."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3739","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3739","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Cabaret Voltaire (Zurich)","value":"Dada","group":"Cabaret Voltaire (Zurich)","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cabaret Voltaire (Zurich)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabaret_Voltaire_%28Zurich%29"}],"explanation74":"The cabaret became a centre of early Dada activity in 1916."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3740","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3740","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q241","subject":"Paranoiac-critical method","value":"Salvador Dalí","group":"Paranoiac-critical method","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"method","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Paranoiac-critical method","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paranoiac-critical_method"}],"explanation74":"Dalí used the method to develop multiple images and irrational associations."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3741","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3741","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q020","subject":"Frottage (art)","value":"Frottage","group":"Frottage (art)","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"technique","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Frottage (art)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frottage_%28art%29"}],"explanation74":"Frottage transfers the texture beneath the paper through rubbing."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3742","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3742","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Grattage","value":"Grattage","group":"Grattage","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"technique","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Grattage","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grattage"}],"explanation74":"Grattage uses scraping as a way of generating marks and textures."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3743","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3743","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Decalcomania","value":"Decalcomania","group":"Decalcomania","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"technique","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Decalcomania","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decalcomania"}],"explanation74":"Decalcomania creates transferred, often unpredictable patterns."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3744","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3744","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q199","subject":"Exquisite corpse","value":"A collaborative composition","group":"Exquisite corpse","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"method","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Exquisite corpse","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exquisite_corpse"}],"explanation74":"Participants contribute successively, often without seeing all preceding contributions."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3745","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3745","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q416","subject":"First Impressionist Exhibition","value":"Nadar","group":"First Impressionist Exhibition","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"setting","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: First Impressionist Exhibition","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Impressionist_Exhibition"}],"explanation74":"The exhibition used premises formerly occupied by the photographer Nadar."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3746","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3746","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q031","subject":"Divisionism","value":"Segantini and Previati","group":"Divisionism","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"association","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Divisionism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divisionism"}],"explanation74":"Segantini and Previati were leading exponents of divided-colour techniques in Italy."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3747","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3747","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q643","subject":"The Fourth Estate (painting)","value":"Striking workers","group":"The Fourth Estate (painting)","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"subject","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: The Fourth Estate (painting)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Fourth_Estate_%28painting%29"}],"explanation74":"The advancing workers give collective labour a monumental presence."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3748","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3748","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q020","subject":"Nabis (art)","value":"Prophets","group":"Nabis (art)","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"term","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Nabis (art)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nabis_%28art%29"}],"explanation74":"The group took its name from a word meaning prophets."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3749","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3749","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Cloisonnism","value":"Dark outlines","group":"Cloisonnism","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"formal analysis","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cloisonnism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloisonnism"}],"explanation74":"The contours suggest the compartments of cloisonné enamel."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3750","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3750","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q470","subject":"Primitivism","value":"Non-Western and archaic art","group":"Primitivism","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Primitivism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primitivism"}],"explanation74":"The term concerns artists' engagement with cultures they labelled primitive, a label now critically examined."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3751","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3751","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Die Brücke","value":"Dresden","group":"Die Brücke","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"location","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Die Brücke","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Die_Br%C3%BCcke"}],"explanation74":"The Expressionist group began in Dresden before members moved to Berlin."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3752","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3752","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q676","subject":"Vienna Secession","value":"An established artists' association","group":"Vienna Secession","major":"Art History","era":"Early 20th century","relation":"institution","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Vienna Secession","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vienna_Secession"}],"explanation74":"Its founders separated from the conservative Vienna artists' association."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3753","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3753","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q560","subject":"Salon des Refusés","value":"Rejection by the official Salon","group":"Salon des Refusés","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"exhibition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Salon des Refusés","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salon_des_Refus%C3%A9s"}],"explanation74":"The exhibition displayed works refused by the official Salon jury."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3754","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3754","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q132","subject":"Encaustic painting","value":"Wax","group":"Encaustic painting","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"technique","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Encaustic painting","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encaustic_painting"}],"explanation74":"Encaustic painting incorporates pigment in heated wax."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3755","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3755","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q199","subject":"Sinopia","value":"A preparatory underdrawing","group":"Sinopia","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"process","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Sinopia","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinopia"}],"explanation74":"The preparatory drawing guides the composition beneath the painted plaster layer."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3756","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3756","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q152","subject":"Giornata","value":"A day's working area","group":"Giornata","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"process","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Giornata","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giornata"}],"explanation74":"The area of fresh plaster is prepared for the amount that can be painted in a day."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3757","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3757","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q199","subject":"Cartoon (art)","value":"A full-scale preparatory drawing","group":"Cartoon (art)","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cartoon (art)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cartoon_%28art%29"}],"explanation74":"A cartoon provides a full-size design for transferring or executing a work."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3758","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3758","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Grisaille","value":"Predominantly grey tones","group":"Grisaille","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"technique","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Grisaille","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grisaille"}],"explanation74":"Grisaille uses monochrome or near-monochrome grey tones, sometimes imitating sculpture."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3759","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3759","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q663","subject":"Tenebrism","value":"Strong light against deep darkness","group":"Tenebrism","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"formal analysis","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Tenebrism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tenebrism"}],"explanation74":"Tenebrism isolates illuminated forms against extensive dark areas."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3760","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3760","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Vanitas","value":"The transience of earthly life","group":"Vanitas","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"iconography","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Vanitas","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanitas"}],"explanation74":"Skulls, extinguished candles and other motifs allude to mortality and fleeting possessions."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3761","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3761","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q295","subject":"Wassily Chair","value":"Tubular steel","group":"Wassily Chair","major":"Design","era":"Early modern","relation":"material context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Wassily Chair","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wassily_Chair"}],"explanation74":"The chair uses tubular steel with flexible seating surfaces, drawing on industrial production."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3762","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3762","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q295","subject":"Panton Chair","value":"A one-piece plastic cantilever form","group":"Panton Chair","major":"Design","era":"Early modern","relation":"innovation","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Panton Chair","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panton_Chair"}],"explanation74":"Panton developed a continuous cantilever chair form in plastic."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3763","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3763","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q295","subject":"No. 14 chair","value":"Steam-bending wood","group":"No. 14 chair","major":"Design","era":"Early modern","relation":"process","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: No. 14 chair","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No._14_chair"}],"explanation74":"Bentwood components allowed the chair to be standardized and efficiently produced."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3764","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3764","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q295","subject":"Sacco chair","value":"A bag filled with beads","group":"Sacco chair","major":"Design","era":"Early modern","relation":"construction","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Sacco chair","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.zanotta.com/en-us/magazine/production/zanotta-sacco-armchair-the-original-icon-of-creative-freedom-since-1968"}],"explanation74":"Its loose filling allows the seat to adapt to the user's body."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3765","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3765","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q644","subject":"Arco (lamp)","value":"Counterweight","group":"Arco (lamp)","major":"Design","era":"20th century","relation":"function","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Arco (lamp)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arco_%28lamp%29"}],"explanation74":"The base balances the long projecting arm that places the light above the user."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3766","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3766","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q295","subject":"Red and Blue Chair","value":"Separate lines and planes","group":"Red and Blue Chair","major":"Design","era":"Early modern","relation":"composition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Red and Blue Chair","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_and_Blue_Chair"}],"explanation74":"Distinct members and planes make the chair's structure visually legible."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3767","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3767","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q646","subject":"Bauhaus","value":"A preliminary course","group":"Bauhaus","major":"Design","era":"20th century","relation":"education","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Bauhaus","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bauhaus"}],"explanation74":"The preliminary course introduced fundamental exploration of materials, form and colour."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3768","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3768","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q080","subject":"Memphis Group","value":"Postmodernism","group":"Memphis Group","major":"Design","era":"Early modern","relation":"classification","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Memphis Group","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memphis_Group"}],"explanation74":"Memphis challenged modernist restraint with strong colours, patterns and unexpected forms."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3769","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3769","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q034","subject":"Strait of Gibraltar","value":"Mediterranean Sea","group":"Strait of Gibraltar","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"connection","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Strait of Gibraltar","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strait_of_Gibraltar"}],"explanation74":"The strait lies between the Iberian Peninsula and North Africa."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3770","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3770","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q034","subject":"Bosporus","value":"Sea of Marmara","group":"Bosporus","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"connection","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Bosporus","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bosporus"}],"explanation74":"The Sea of Marmara lies between the Bosporus and the Dardanelles."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3771","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3771","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q244","subject":"Dardanelles","value":"Aegean Sea","group":"Dardanelles","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"connection","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Dardanelles","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dardanelles"}],"explanation74":"The Dardanelles forms the western maritime connection from the Sea of Marmara."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3772","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3772","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q014","subject":"Suez Canal","value":"Red Sea","group":"Suez Canal","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"connection","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Suez Canal","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suez_Canal"}],"explanation74":"The canal provides a route between the Mediterranean and the Red Sea."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3773","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3773","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q085","subject":"Panama Canal","value":"Pacific Ocean","group":"Panama Canal","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"connection","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Panama Canal","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panama_Canal"}],"explanation74":"The canal crosses the Isthmus of Panama between the Atlantic and Pacific systems."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3774","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3774","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q014","subject":"Danube","value":"Black Sea","group":"Danube","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"river mouth","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Danube","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danube"}],"explanation74":"The Danube reaches the Black Sea through its delta."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3775","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3775","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q014","subject":"Rhine","value":"North Sea","group":"Rhine","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"river mouth","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Rhine","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhine"}],"explanation74":"The Rhine flows through western Europe towards the North Sea."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3776","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3776","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q034","subject":"Caspian Sea","value":"An enclosed inland body of water","group":"Caspian Sea","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"physical geography","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Caspian Sea","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caspian_Sea"}],"explanation74":"Despite its name, the Caspian has no natural outlet to the ocean."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3777","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3777","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q014","subject":"Atlas Mountains","value":"North-west Africa","group":"Atlas Mountains","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"location","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Atlas Mountains","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlas_Mountains"}],"explanation74":"The range extends across Morocco, Algeria and Tunisia."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3778","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3778","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q014","subject":"Dinaric Alps","value":"Adriatic Sea","group":"Dinaric Alps","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"location","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Dinaric Alps","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dinaric_Alps"}],"explanation74":"The mountain system extends through the western Balkan Peninsula."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3779","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3779","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q085","subject":"Mesopotamia","value":"Tigris and Euphrates","group":"Mesopotamia","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"region","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Mesopotamia","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesopotamia"}],"explanation74":"The Greek name means land between rivers and refers chiefly to the Tigris–Euphrates region."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3780","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3780","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q014","subject":"Ural Mountains","value":"Ural Mountains","group":"Ural Mountains","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"boundary","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ural Mountains","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ural_Mountains"}],"explanation74":"The Urals form part of the conventional geographical division between the two continents."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3781","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3781","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q014","subject":"Sahel","value":"Sahara","group":"Sahel","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"region","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Sahel","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sahel"}],"explanation74":"The Sahel forms a semi-arid belt between the Sahara and wetter savannas to the south."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3782","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3782","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q085","subject":"Gulf Stream","value":"North Atlantic","group":"Gulf Stream","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"oceanography","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Gulf Stream","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulf_Stream"}],"explanation74":"The current system carries warm water northward and eastward in the Atlantic."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3783","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3783","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q169","subject":"Rosetta Stone","value":"Ancient Greek","group":"Rosetta Stone","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"language","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Rosetta Stone","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosetta_Stone"}],"explanation74":"The decree appears in Egyptian scripts and in Greek, enabling comparison."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3784","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3784","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q022","subject":"Code of Hammurabi","value":"Babylonia","group":"Code of Hammurabi","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"civilisation","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Code of Hammurabi","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_of_Hammurabi"}],"explanation74":"Hammurabi ruled Babylon in the eighteenth century BCE."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3785","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3785","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q022","subject":"Library of Ashurbanipal","value":"Nineveh","group":"Library of Ashurbanipal","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"location","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Library of Ashurbanipal","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library_of_Ashurbanipal"}],"explanation74":"Its cuneiform tablets formed a major royal collection in the Assyrian capital."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3786","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3786","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q126","subject":"Achaemenid Empire","value":"Achaemenid","group":"Achaemenid Empire","major":"History & Politics","era":"Medieval","relation":"dynasty","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Achaemenid Empire","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achaemenid_Empire"}],"explanation74":"Alexander's conquest ended the Achaemenid Empire ruled by Darius III."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3787","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3787","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q211","subject":"Ptolemaic Kingdom","value":"Egypt","group":"Ptolemaic Kingdom","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"territory","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ptolemaic Kingdom","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ptolemaic_Kingdom"}],"explanation74":"The Ptolemies ruled Egypt from Alexandria until the Roman conquest."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3788","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3788","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q103","subject":"Tribune of the plebs","value":"Tribunes of the plebs","group":"Tribune of the plebs","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"institution","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Tribune of the plebs","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tribune_of_the_plebs"}],"explanation74":"The tribunes could intervene against actions of other magistrates."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3789","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3789","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q057","subject":"Twelve Tables","value":"To make laws accessible","group":"Twelve Tables","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"purpose","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Twelve Tables","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve_Tables"}],"explanation74":"Written public laws reduced the exclusive control of legal knowledge by elites."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3790","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3790","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q109","subject":"Social War (91–87 BC)","value":"Roman citizenship","group":"Social War (91–87 BC)","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"cause","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Social War (91–87 BC)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_War_%2891%E2%80%9387_BC%29"}],"explanation74":"Many Italian allies sought access to the citizenship held by Romans."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3791","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3791","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q095","subject":"Battle of Tours","value":"Franks","group":"Battle of Tours","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"participants","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Battle of Tours","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Tours"}],"explanation74":"Charles Martel led Frankish forces against an Umayyad army."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3792","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3792","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Domesday Book","value":"Landholdings and resources","group":"Domesday Book","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"purpose","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Domesday Book","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domesday_Book"}],"explanation74":"The survey recorded land, its holders and economic resources in much of England."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3793","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3793","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Guild","value":"Apprentice → journeyman → master","group":"Guild","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"sequence","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Guild","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guild"}],"explanation74":"An apprentice learned the trade before working as a journeyman and potentially becoming a master."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3794","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3794","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q095","subject":"Battle of Hattin","value":"Jerusalem","group":"Battle of Hattin","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"consequence","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Battle of Hattin","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Hattin"}],"explanation74":"The defeat of the crusader army opened the way to the capture of Jerusalem."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3795","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3795","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q493","subject":"War of the Spanish Succession","value":"Charles II of Spain","group":"War of the Spanish Succession","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"cause","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: War of the Spanish Succession","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_of_the_Spanish_Succession"}],"explanation74":"Charles II's death in 1700 created a disputed succession to the Spanish monarchy."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3796","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3796","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q040","subject":"Treaty of Tordesillas","value":"Portugal and Castile","group":"Treaty of Tordesillas","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"participants","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Treaty of Tordesillas","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Tordesillas"}],"explanation74":"The 1494 agreement drew a demarcation line for Portuguese and Castilian expansion."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3797","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3797","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q079","subject":"Price revolution","value":"Sustained inflation","group":"Price revolution","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Price revolution","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Price_revolution"}],"explanation74":"Rising prices were associated with demographic change and increased monetary supply, including American silver."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3798","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3798","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Mercantilism","value":"Promoting exports and limiting imports","group":"Mercantilism","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"policy","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Mercantilism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercantilism"}],"explanation74":"Mercantilist policies sought to strengthen the state through controlled trade and favourable balances."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3799","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3799","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q160","subject":"Glorious Revolution","value":"William III and Mary II","group":"Glorious Revolution","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"succession","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Glorious Revolution","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glorious_Revolution"}],"explanation74":"William and Mary replaced James II following the events of 1688."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3800","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3800","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q022","subject":"Bill of Rights 1689","value":"Parliament","group":"Bill of Rights 1689","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"institution","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Bill of Rights 1689","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_of_Rights_1689"}],"explanation74":"The settlement restricted royal powers and affirmed parliamentary rights."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3801","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3801","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q494","subject":"Seven Years' War","value":"Seven Years' War","group":"Seven Years' War","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"reaty","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Seven Years' War","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven_Years%27_War"}],"explanation74":"The 1763 settlement reshaped colonial possessions after the global Seven Years' War."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3802","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3802","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q394","subject":"Constitutional Convention (United States)","value":"Philadelphia","group":"Constitutional Convention (United States)","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"location","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Constitutional Convention (United States)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitutional_Convention_%28United_States%29"}],"explanation74":"Delegates drafted the Constitution at the Philadelphia Convention."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3803","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3803","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q189","subject":"United States Bill of Rights","value":"Ten","group":"United States Bill of Rights","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"institution","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: United States Bill of Rights","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Bill_of_Rights"}],"explanation74":"The first ten amendments, ratified in 1791, form the Bill of Rights."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3804","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3804","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q456","subject":"Estates General (France)","value":"Clergy","group":"Estates General (France)","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"social structure","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Estates General (France)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estates_General_%28France%29"}],"explanation74":"The clergy formed the First Estate, the nobility the Second, and the remaining population the Third."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3805","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3805","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q148","subject":"Tennis Court Oath","value":"To establish a constitution","group":"Tennis Court Oath","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"objective","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Tennis Court Oath","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tennis_Court_Oath"}],"explanation74":"They pledged not to disperse before giving France a constitution."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3806","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3806","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Thermidorian Reaction","value":"Robespierre","group":"Thermidorian Reaction","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"turning point","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Thermidorian Reaction","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thermidorian_Reaction"}],"explanation74":"Robespierre's overthrow in July 1794 marked the end of the most radical phase of the Terror."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3807","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3807","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q122","subject":"Napoleonic Code","value":"Civil law","group":"Napoleonic Code","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"law","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Napoleonic Code","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleonic_Code"}],"explanation74":"The code organized civil law, including property and family relations."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3808","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3808","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q079","subject":"Continental System","value":"Britain","group":"Continental System","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","relation":"objective","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Continental System","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continental_System"}],"explanation74":"The blockade aimed to exclude British trade from continental Europe."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3809","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3809","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q126","subject":"Decembrist revolt","value":"Russian Empire","group":"Decembrist revolt","major":"History & Politics","era":"Medieval","relation":"location","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Decembrist revolt","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decembrist_revolt"}],"explanation74":"Russian officers challenged the autocratic order during the succession crisis of 1825."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3810","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3810","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q061","subject":"Peterloo Massacre","value":"Parliamentary reform","group":"Peterloo Massacre","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"cause","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Peterloo Massacre","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peterloo_Massacre"}],"explanation74":"The Manchester gathering demanded political representation and reform."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3811","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3811","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"The Communist Manifesto","value":"Marx and Engels","group":"The Communist Manifesto","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"authorship","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: The Communist Manifesto","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Communist_Manifesto"}],"explanation74":"Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels published the manifesto in 1848."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3812","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3812","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q118","subject":"Revolutions of 1848","value":"Metternich","group":"Revolutions of 1848","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"consequence","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Revolutions of 1848","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revolutions_of_1848"}],"explanation74":"Metternich's fall symbolized the challenge to the conservative order established after Napoleon."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3813","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3813","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q040","subject":"Treaty of Nanking","value":"Hong Kong Island","group":"Treaty of Nanking","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"territorial change","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Treaty of Nanking","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Nanking"}],"explanation74":"The treaty ending the First Opium War ceded Hong Kong Island to Britain."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3814","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3814","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q479","subject":"Meiji Restoration","value":"The emperor","group":"Meiji Restoration","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","relation":"regime change","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Meiji Restoration","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meiji_Restoration"}],"explanation74":"The restoration ended Tokugawa shogunal rule and inaugurated the Meiji era."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3815","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3815","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Berlin Conference","value":"Africa","group":"Berlin Conference","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Berlin Conference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berlin_Conference"}],"explanation74":"The conference established rules for European colonial claims and trade in Africa."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3816","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3816","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q023","subject":"Congress of Berlin","value":"Ottoman Empire","group":"Congress of Berlin","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Congress of Berlin","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congress_of_Berlin"}],"explanation74":"It revised the Treaty of San Stefano and reorganized arrangements in the Balkans."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3817","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3817","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Boxer Rebellion","value":"Foreign influence and Christianity","group":"Boxer Rebellion","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"objective","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Boxer Rebellion","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxer_Rebellion"}],"explanation74":"The movement opposed foreign interference and Christian missionary activity."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3818","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3818","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Dreyfus affair","value":"Émile Zola","group":"Dreyfus affair","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"authorship","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Dreyfus affair","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dreyfus_affair"}],"explanation74":"Zola publicly challenged the handling of the case against Alfred Dreyfus."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3819","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3819","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q456","subject":"Entente Cordiale","value":"Britain and France","group":"Entente Cordiale","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"participants","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Entente Cordiale","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entente_Cordiale"}],"explanation74":"The agreements settled several colonial disputes between Britain and France."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3820","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3820","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Schlieffen Plan","value":"Belgium","group":"Schlieffen Plan","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"cause","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Schlieffen Plan","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schlieffen_Plan"}],"explanation74":"Germany's invasion of Belgium in August 1914 was central to Britain's entry into the war."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3821","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3821","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q023","subject":"Treaty of Brest-Litovsk","value":"Soviet Russia","group":"Treaty of Brest-Litovsk","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"participants","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Treaty of Brest-Litovsk","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Brest-Litovsk"}],"explanation74":"The Bolshevik government signed the treaty with the Central Powers in 1918."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3822","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3822","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Fourteen Points","value":"Woodrow Wilson","group":"Fourteen Points","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"authorship","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Fourteen Points","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourteen_Points"}],"explanation74":"Wilson presented the Fourteen Points in January 1918."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3823","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3823","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q126","subject":"League of Nations mandate","value":"Germany and the Ottoman Empire","group":"League of Nations mandate","major":"History & Politics","era":"Medieval","relation":"territories","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: League of Nations mandate","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/League_of_Nations_mandate"}],"explanation74":"Mandates covered former German colonies and former Ottoman territories."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3824","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3824","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q040","subject":"Treaty of Lausanne","value":"Treaty of Sèvres","group":"Treaty of Lausanne","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"treaty","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Treaty of Lausanne","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Lausanne"}],"explanation74":"Lausanne replaced the unimplemented Treaty of Sèvres as the basis of the settlement with Turkey."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3825","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3825","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q683","subject":"Munich Agreement","value":"Czechoslovakia","group":"Munich Agreement","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"exception","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Munich Agreement","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munich_Agreement"}],"explanation74":"Czechoslovakia was excluded even though the settlement concerned its territory."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3826","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3826","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q491","subject":"Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact","value":"Non-aggression pact","group":"Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact","major":"History & Politics","era":"Medieval","relation":"treaty type","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molotov%E2%80%93Ribbentrop_Pact"}],"explanation74":"The 1939 agreement publicly pledged non-aggression; secret protocols addressed spheres of influence."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3827","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3827","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q455","subject":"Wannsee Conference","value":"Genocide of European Jews","group":"Wannsee Conference","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Wannsee Conference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wannsee_Conference"}],"explanation74":"The conference coordinated implementation of the Holocaust, not the beginning of Nazi persecution."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3828","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3828","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q022","subject":"United Nations Conference on International Organization","value":"San Francisco","group":"United Nations Conference on International Organization","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"location","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: United Nations Conference on International Organization","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Conference_on_International_Organization"}],"explanation74":"The Charter was signed at the San Francisco conference before entering into force later in 1945."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3829","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3829","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Bretton Woods Conference","value":"IMF and World Bank","group":"Bretton Woods Conference","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"institutions","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Bretton Woods Conference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bretton_Woods_Conference"}],"explanation74":"The agreements created the IMF and the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3830","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3830","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Bandung Conference","value":"Asia and Africa","group":"Bandung Conference","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"participants","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Bandung Conference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bandung_Conference"}],"explanation74":"Bandung brought Asian and African countries together around cooperation and decolonization."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3831","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3831","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q022","subject":"Détente","value":"Easing of tensions","group":"Détente","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"definition","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Détente","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9tente"}],"explanation74":"Détente denotes a relaxation of strained relations, especially between major powers."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3832","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3832","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Strategic Arms Limitation Talks","value":"Strategic nuclear arms","group":"Strategic Arms Limitation Talks","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"policy","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Strategic Arms Limitation Talks","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Arms_Limitation_Talks"}],"explanation74":"SALT addressed strategic nuclear delivery systems and related restrictions."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3833","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3833","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q684","subject":"Solidarity (Polish trade union)","value":"An independent trade union","group":"Solidarity (Polish trade union)","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","relation":"institution","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Solidarity (Polish trade union)","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solidarity_%28Polish_trade_union%29"}],"explanation74":"The trade-union movement became a major challenge to communist rule in Poland."},{"id":"ORIGINAL80-3834","nodeId":"ORIGINAL80-3834","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q079","subject":"Velvet Revolution","value":"Czechoslovakia","group":"Velvet Revolution","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","relation":"location","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Velvet Revolution","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wik